1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,093
Lesson 4.6.

2
00:00:02,473 --> 00:00:04,842
Hopfield networks and associative memory.

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:09,557
Your memory does not look up memories
by address, like a hard drive.

4
00:00:09,937 --> 00:00:15,331
A fragment, a smell or half a face
is enough to recover the full memory.

5
00:00:15,711 --> 00:00:20,256
In 1982, John Hopfield showed
that an Ising model at zero

6
00:00:20,256 --> 00:00:25,519
temperature does exactly that: it
is a content-addressable memory.

7
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:29,207
The idea is to sculpt
the energy landscape so

8
00:00:29,207 --> 00:00:31,651
that each memory is
the bottom of a valley.

9
00:00:32,031 --> 00:00:35,357
An incomplete or noisy cue
is a point on the slope.

10
00:00:35,737 --> 00:00:37,952
Letting the system roll
down is remembering.

11
00:00:38,332 --> 00:00:41,060
The more memories we store,
the more valleys there

12
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,625
are, and at some point
they start to interfere.

13
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:50,615
To store the patterns ξ^μ
we use the Hebb rule: the

14
00:00:50,615 --> 00:00:56,940
couplings are J = (1/N) Σ
ξ^μ ξ^μᵀ, with zero diagonal.

15
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:00,230
Neurons that fire together
get wired together.

16
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:06,111
To recall, each spin aligns with
its local field: s_i ← sgn(u_i).

17
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:08,435
It is the pbit with infinite β.

18
00:01:08,815 --> 00:01:11,966
Each of those updates
never increases the Ising

19
00:01:11,966 --> 00:01:15,184
energy, so the network
always ends in a minimum.

20
00:01:16,317 --> 00:01:20,195
Let's store three hundred-pixel patterns
in a network of a hundred neurons.

21
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:24,825
Take one of them and flip twenty
percent of its pixels at random.

22
00:01:25,205 --> 00:01:29,207
As the dynamics run, the pixels are
corrected one by one, and within

23
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:32,738
a few sweeps the network recovers
the full original pattern.

24
00:01:33,118 --> 00:01:36,189
The noisy cue was in the
right basin of attraction.

25
00:01:37,317 --> 00:01:38,620
How many memories fit?

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,092
Each spin's local field has a
signal part, from the target

27
00:01:43,092 --> 00:01:46,691
pattern, and a crosstalk
part, from all the others.

28
00:01:47,071 --> 00:01:51,701
As long as the load α = P/N
is small, the signal wins.

29
00:01:52,081 --> 00:01:56,292
Around α ≈ 0.14 the
crosstalk dominates and

30
00:01:56,292 --> 00:02:00,308
the memory collapses: memories get mixed.

31
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:04,459
Spurious states also appear,
minima that correspond to no

32
00:02:04,459 --> 00:02:07,833
stored pattern, such as
mixtures of three memories.

33
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:14,737
With four hundred neurons and cues with
ten percent noise, the final overlap

34
00:02:14,737 --> 00:02:20,068
with the correct pattern is practically
one for loads of 0.05 and 0.1.

35
00:02:20,448 --> 00:02:26,479
At 0.16 it falls to 0.83,
and at 0.2, to 0.55.

36
00:02:26,859 --> 00:02:30,580
Four hundred neurons store
about fifty reliable memories.

37
00:02:31,683 --> 00:02:36,495
At positive temperature, the same
network built with pbits samples around

38
00:02:36,495 --> 00:02:40,910
the memories, and that is the
starting point of Boltzmann machines.

39
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:45,305
Modern Hopfield networks have
exponential capacity and an

40
00:02:45,305 --> 00:02:48,755
update rule that resembles
transformer attention.

41
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:53,839
And in 2024, Hopfield received the
Nobel Prize in Physics for these ideas.

42
00:02:54,950 --> 00:03:01,023
Common mistakes: leaving a nonzero diagonal,
updating all neurons at once, which

43
00:03:01,023 --> 00:03:06,413
can cause oscillations, overloading
the memory beyond fourteen percent,

44
00:03:06,793 --> 00:03:10,496
and storing highly correlated
patterns, which interfere more.

45
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:16,445
In summary: a Hopfield network is an
Ising model with Hebbian couplings.

46
00:03:16,825 --> 00:03:19,626
Its energy minima are the memories, and

47
00:03:19,626 --> 00:03:22,930
remembering is descending
with s_i ← sgn(u_i).

48
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:26,968
The capacity is about 0.14
N patterns, and beyond

49
00:03:26,968 --> 00:03:30,328
that interference and
spurious states appear.

50
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:34,583
To practice: prove that the
energy does not increase.

51
00:03:34,963 --> 00:03:37,787
Compute the local field with
a single stored pattern.

52
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:40,947
Estimate the capacity of
a thousand-neuron network.

53
00:03:41,327 --> 00:03:44,475
And explain why the inverted
pattern is also a minimum.
